<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Licata</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Licata is a town on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, at the mouth of the Salso river. It succeeds the Greek city founded around 282 BCE by Phintias, tyrant of Acragas, who resettled the people of Gela there and named it after himself. In 256 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> won the naval Battle of Cape Ecnomus off its coast during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/licata.jpg" alt="Licata" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Davide Mauro · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_di_Licata_da_Castel_Sant%27Angelo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Licata is a city and comune on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, at the mouth of the Salso River, which in antiquity was the Himera, about midway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agrigento">Agrigento</a> and Gela. It is a seaport that ships sulphur and asphalt, and sulphur refining made it a major European exporting centre. West of the port a series of pocket beaches is separated by wave-cut headlands as high as 40 metres. The ancient Greek city stood on the right bank of the river, protected by the headland now named Monte San Michele, beside a small natural harbour about 80 metres across that has since been infilled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> traded at the site between the 12th and 8th centuries BCE. At the end of the 7th century BCE settlers from Gela built a fortified station to guard the river mouth, and in the first half of the 6th century BCE Phalaris, tyrant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agrigento">Agrigento</a>, built an outpost.</p><p>In 311 BCE Agathocles was beaten by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> at the Battle of the Himera River near the town, which then fell to them. The city was refounded in 282 BCE by Phintias, tyrant of Agrigentum, who razed Gela, resettled its people and named the new city for himself. It was laid out on a great scale, with walls, temples and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agora">agora</a>, but never rose to the importance of Gela; as late as the 1st century BCE, inscriptions and coins show that its inhabitants still called themselves Geloi.</p><p>In 256 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> won the Battle of Cape Ecnomus nearby in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-punic-war">First Punic War</a> and freed the city from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a>. Cicero describes it as a seaport with a considerable export trade in corn. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> conquered Licata in 827, and the Normans took it on 25 July 1086. In 1270 it rebelled against Angevin rule in the Sicilian Vespers. Corsairs under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turgut-reis">Dragut</a> sacked it in 1553, after which the walls were rebuilt. In the 1870s two bridges to the inland sulphur mines and five refineries brought economic expansion. Licata served as an Allied landing point in 1943 during the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Museo Civico displays archaeological finds from burial grounds dating from prehistoric times to the 3rd century BCE. Among the sights are the remains of the ancient Greek city on Monte Sant'Angelo, the necropolis of Monte Petrulla, the 17th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/castel-santangelo">Castel Sant'Angelo</a> and the Palazzo di Città, designed by Ernesto Basile. John Hersey's novel A Bell for Adano took Licata as its model.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Licata” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Timoleon resettled it in the 330s BCE, but in 282 BCE Phintias, tyrant of Acragas, destroyed it and moved its people to his new city at <b>Licata</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/gela">Gela</a></li></ul></section>
